<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Merged into the Info.plist that Tauri generates at bundle time. Only the keys
  Tauri does not already set belong here; everything about the window, version,
  and identifier is generated from tauri.conf.json and must not be duplicated.

  These are TCC usage descriptions. Under the hardened runtime they are not
  cosmetic: they are the sentence the operating system shows a stranger the
  first time the app reaches for their files, and that prompt is roughly the
  fourth thing that happens after a download. A missing string means macOS
  supplies a generic one that explains nothing, on a security-positioned product
  whose entire pitch is that you can see what it does.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and why, so nobody adds it back on a hunch:

    NSCameraUsageDescription
      Nothing captures video. No `getUserMedia({video})` anywhere in assets/js.

    NSAppleEventsUsageDescription
      The app scripts nothing. `osascript` appears only under scripts/, which
      runs outside the bundle and therefore outside this entitlement's reach.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- The default workspace for a fresh install is ~/Desktop/BusterClawCLI
       (desktop/tauri/src/workspace.rs). macOS gates the Desktop folder, so this
       prompt fires on first run for essentially every new user. It is the first
       thing the app ever asks for. -->
  <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw keeps your workspace — the notes, documents, and shift logs your agent reads and writes — in a folder on your Desktop. It only touches that folder.</string>

  <!-- The workspace root is user-choosable, so the same prompt can arrive for
       either of these instead. Same sentence, same promise. -->
  <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw keeps your workspace — the notes, documents, and shift logs your agent reads and writes — in the folder you chose. It only touches that folder.</string>

  <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw saves files your agent downloads to this folder, and reads the ones you point it at.</string>

  <!-- ADDED 09-02-26, correcting a comment that had been false since 08-16.
       This block used to list the microphone as "deliberately absent" on the
       grounds that "there is no getUserMedia anywhere in assets/js". There is:
       assets/js/hooks/voice_recorder.js calls it twice (lines 117 and 123).

       This is not a cosmetic omission. Under the hardened runtime an app that
       reaches for the microphone with NO usage string is TERMINATED by TCC — not
       shown a generic prompt. So the first person to click record would have
       watched the app die and reasonably concluded the webview cannot capture,
       when the actual cause was one missing line in this file. That wrong
       conclusion is the expensive part; the fix is here.

       UNRESOLVED, and worth knowing before trusting either answer: whether an
       arbitrary page in the embedded browser can also reach the microphone now
       that the key exists. The comment removed above asserted Tauri's
       WKUIDelegate denies capture requests before TCC is consulted. The browser
       roadmap's finding asserts the opposite — that wry owns the single
       uiDelegate slot and AUTO-GRANTS camera and mic. Both cannot be true, and
       neither has been exercised in a packaged build. If wry auto-grants, this
       key changes what a browsed page can do, and that is a security question
       rather than a plist question. -->
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Buster Claw records audio only when you press record, and only into your own workspace folder. Nothing is sent anywhere — recording and playback both happen on this Mac.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
